// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/trap/irq.vuma
// VWK IRQ Subsystem — IRQ ring-buffer dispatcher + handler table (PMT-pure).
// Wave R2f — migrated onto the IRQ ring buffer (R2e).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the IRQ subsystem. It maintains a 256-entry IRQ handler
// table (one slot per interrupt vector 0..255, each slot holding a u64
// handler-function address or 0 if unregistered) AND a 32-entry lock-free
// IRQ ring buffer (R2e — the asm trampoline pushes; the dispatcher pops).
// The new top-level entry point is `irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl)`: a
// tight drain loop that pops vectors off the ring until it sees the empty
// sentinel (9999), and for each vector looks up the handler in the table
// and (if registered) sends EOI to the PIC.
//
// This R2f update replaces K3d's old single-IRQ `irq_dispatch(tbl, irq)`
// (which took a single irq number from the trap frame's vector field)
// with the ring-draining `irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl)`. The old
// synchronous model — trap_handler → trap_irq → irq_dispatch(tbl, vector)
// — is replaced by the decoupled model — asm trampoline → irq_ring_push,
// then trap_handler → trap_irq → irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl). The
// decoupling lets the asm trampoline stay short (just the push, ~20
// cycles) and defer all the heavy dispatch logic to the PMT dispatcher
// running on a clean stack with full GPRs saved.
//
// ── IRQ handler lifecycle (R2f model) ───────────────────────────────────────
//   1. kmain allocates an IrqRing + an IrqTable via state_new, zero-inits
//      both (irq_ring_init(ring); tbl.count = 0). [K11 — early boot]
//   2. Drivers call register_irq(tbl, irq, handler) to install a handler
//      for their IRQ line. register_irq:
//        a. Packs the handler address into tbl.handlers[irq*8..irq*8+8]
//           via irq_set_handler.
//        b. Increments tbl.count.
//        c. Calls the irq_unmask(irq) extern to clear the IRQ's mask bit
//           at the 8259 PIC, allowing the PIC to deliver that IRQ.
//   3. On a hardware interrupt, the asm trampoline (R2b/R2c/R2d's
//      trap_trampoline.vuma) saves the native save area, calls the per-
//      arch trap_to_superset adapter to populate a superset TrapFrame,
//      and pushes the IRQ vector into the per-CPU IrqRing via
//      `irq_ring_push(ring, vector)`. The trampoline then returns (the
//      PIC is still asserting the IRQ until EOI, but the dispatcher will
//      EOI it shortly).
//   4. The PMT dispatcher (trap_handler in trap.vuma, called from the
//      trap return path with full TrapFrame saved + interrupts re-
//      enabled) routes IRQ vectors (anything not in [0,32) and not 128)
//      to trap_irq, which (in K11) calls
//      `irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl)` from this file. The loop:
//        a. Pops a vector via `v = irq_ring_pop(ring)`.
//        b. If v == 9999 (empty sentinel): return — ring drained.
//        c. Else: look up the handler via `irq_get_handler(tbl, v as u8)`.
//           If non-zero: send EOI via `pic_eoi(v as u8)` (simplified —
//           K11 adds the real fn-pointer handler call before the EOI).
//           If zero (spurious IRQ): still send EOI (the 8259 PIC will
//           re-deliver spurious IRQ 7 / 15 if you don't EOI them).
//        d. Repeat from (a).
//   5. Drivers can also enable/disable an IRQ on the fly via
//      enable_irq(irq) / disable_irq(irq) (thin wrappers around the
//      irq_unmask / irq_mask externs).
//
// ── Why a ring-draining loop instead of single-IRQ dispatch? ────────────────
//   The old K3d `irq_dispatch(tbl, irq)` was called once per trap with a
//   single vector. If multiple IRQs were pending (a common case under
//   load — e.g. the timer fires while a NIC IRQ is being serviced), the
//   asm trampoline would push multiple entries into the ring and the
//   dispatcher would have to be called once per pending IRQ. The new
//   `irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl)` drains the entire ring in one call,
//   so the trap return path invokes the dispatcher once and the loop
//   handles all pending IRQs. This is the lock-free SPSC pattern R2e
//   established: the asm trampoline is the single producer (running
//   interrupts-off), the dispatcher is the single consumer (running
//   with interrupts re-enabled after the trap return path saved the
//   full TrapFrame). See irq_ring.vuma's header for the SPSC invariant.
//
// ── Why is the handler-call still a stub (just EOI)? ────────────────────────
//   VUMA has no function-pointer call today: `handler()` where `handler`
//   is a u64 won't compile (the parser only accepts direct fn-identifier
//   calls, not indirect calls through a u64). The contract's "Note" for
//   R2f says: "look up handler in IrqTable for vector v (simplified: just
//   call pic_eoi if registered)". So the loop:
//     - reads the handler (via irq_get_handler) — exercises the pack/unpack
//     - branches on handler != 0 (both branches just call pic_eoi)
//     - sends EOI
//   This is NOT a violation of the "no stubs" rule because the DoD
//   explicitly documents this as a stub for K11 to replace. The hosted-
//   mode path never actually delivers hardware IRQs (the host kernel
//   owns the real IRQ delivery), so the stub's no-op behavior is correct
//   for hosted mode.
//
// ── Why re-declare the irq_mask / irq_unmask / pic_eoi externs? ─────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the three
// extern "C" trampolines byte-identically to K3a's
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap_trampoline.vuma. (K3a's file declares FIVE
// externs — idt_load, irq_mask, irq_unmask, pic_eoi, cr2_read — but this
// file only needs the three IRQ-related ones.) The byte-identical-
// redeclaration invariant (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay byte-
// identical") extends to extern declarations too: this file's
// `irq_mask(irq: u8)` MUST see the same SysV AMD64 RDI argument as K3a's
// `irq_mask(irq: u8)`.
//
// Hosted: these resolve to __ffi_fallback_stub (returns 0, void calls
//         no-op) — so register_irq's irq_unmask and irq_dispatch_loop's
//         pic_eoi are no-ops in hosted mode. Fine for self-test.
// Bare-metal (K11): real asm stubs registered in
//         src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs:
//     irq_mask(irq)    — K11 stub: `mov al, 0xFF; out 0x21, al` (mask
//                        all — refined in K11 to per-IRQ bit-masking via
//                        `mov al, ~(1 << irq); out 0x21, al`).
//     irq_unmask(irq)  — K11 stub: `mov al, 0xFE; out 0x21, al` (unmask
//                        IRQ 0 — refined in K11 to per-IRQ bit-clearing).
//     pic_eoi(irq)     — K11 stub: `mov al, 0x20; out 0x20, al; ret`
//                        (non-specific EOI to the master PIC's command
//                        port — required after every hardware IRQ).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// IrqTable is a 2052-byte struct (2048-byte handlers array + 4-byte
// count) held in a `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<IrqTable>; the IrqRing
// is a 268-byte struct (256-byte buf + 3 × 4-byte cursors) held in a
// separate State<IrqRing>. The CALLER allocates both via state_new and
// passes them by reference to register_irq / irq_dispatch_loop /
// irq_get_handler / irq_set_handler / irq_ring_init / irq_ring_push /
// irq_ring_pop (init-style API — see "Why init-style?" in
// womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma for the codegen rationale). The enable_irq /
// disable_irq helpers don't take a State<T> at all — they're thin
// wrappers around the irq_unmask / irq_mask externs (each takes only the
// irq number as a u8). The State<IrqTable> + State<IrqRing> params to
// the helpers are regular VUMA-fn borrows (NOT extern #[borrow] — VUMA
// fn calls don't need the attribute, only extern "C" calls do).
// register_irq and irq_dispatch_loop DO call externs (irq_unmask, pic_eoi)
// — but the State<IrqTable> + State<IrqRing> are preserved across the
// extern call because the codegen's conservative invalidation only fires
// on extern #[borrow] State<T> params, and neither IrqTable nor IrqRing
// is passed as an extern #[borrow] (the externs don't take them at all).
// So there is no use-after-invalidate risk.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares the IrqRing layout +
// irq_ring_init / irq_ring_push / irq_ring_pop byte-identically to R2e's
// womb/kernel/trap/irq_ring.vuma (the byte-identical-re-declaration
// invariant — same convention as R2a's TrapFrame being re-declared
// byte-identically by R2b/R2c/R2d's per-arch trap_trampoline.vuma files
// and by R2f's trap.vuma dispatcher). It re-declares the IrqTable layout
// + irq_get_handler / irq_set_handler byte-identically to K3d's pre-R2f
// irq.vuma (the layout is unchanged — only the top-level dispatch entry
// point changes from `irq_dispatch(tbl, irq)` to
// `irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl)`). A mismatch in field order or type
// would silently produce different field offsets at the call site — see
// kernel-developer-guide.md §9 "Byte-identical re-declaration
// invariant".
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K3d / R2f
// contract's "Use decimal constants" rule:
//     2048 = 0x800   256 entries × 8 bytes (IrqTable.handlers array size)
//     4096 = 0x1000  the dummy handler address used in the self-test —
//                    also confirms u64 round-trip through pack/unpack for
//                    a value larger than 255, catching any accidental
//                    u8 truncation in the codegen's [u8; N] access
//                    lowering — same rationale as K3a's rip=4096 check
//     256  = 0x100   IrqRing.buf array size (32 × 8 bytes) — byte-loop
//                    bound
//     32   = 0x20    IrqRing capacity (32 entries) + PIC-IRQ base offset
//                    (the 8259 PIC's IRQs are remapped to vectors 32..47
//                    by the IDT setup in K11's boot path on x86_64)
//     31   = 0x1f    wrap mask for the 32-entry IrqRing (`(head + 1) & 31`)
//     9999 = 0x270f  IrqRing empty sentinel — out-of-range for any
//                    arch's IRQ vector space (x86_64 0..255, aarch64 EC
//                    0..63, riscv64 scause 0..63 in synchronous space)
//                    — see irq_ring.vuma's header for the rationale
//     8    = 0x8     bytes per u64 handler entry (loop bound for
//                    pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF    byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path
//     0 - 1 = -1     IrqRing full sentinel — VUMA's `0 - 1` idiom for
//                    -1 (see waitq.vuma's header for the codegen
//                    rationale)
//     33, 34 = 0x21, 0x22  self-test IRQ vectors (alongside 32)
// Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-
// extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for
// the same rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule).
//
// ── Why `(irq as u32) * 8` and not `irq * 8`? ───────────────────────────────
// `irq` is a u8. The codegen's binary-multiply on u8 would compute
// `(u8 * u8) as u32`, which overflows for irq >= 32 (32 * 8 = 256 > 255).
// The `(irq as u32) * 8` form widens FIRST, then multiplies — producing
// a u32 offset that won't overflow until irq = 2^29 (way beyond the 256-
// entry table). This is the same pattern pmm.vuma's pool_get_base uses
// (`idx * 8` where idx is already u32 — pmm.vuma gets away with it
// because its caller passes u32; irq.vuma's caller passes u8, so the
// explicit cast is needed). Verified in probe before touching the kernel
// tree (probe_irq.vuma — IVE Pass, exit=0).
//
// ── Why `v as u8` in irq_dispatch_loop? ─────────────────────────────────────
// `v` is the u64 vector popped off the IrqRing. The IrqTable's helpers
// (irq_get_handler, irq_set_handler) and the pic_eoi extern all take
// `irq: u8`. The `v as u8` cast narrows the u64 to u8 — safe because
// valid IRQ vectors are 0..255 (x86_64) or 0..63 (aarch64/riscv64), all
// of which fit in a u8. The 9999 empty sentinel is checked BEFORE the
// cast, so it never reaches the narrowing path. This is the same
// `as u8` narrowing pattern K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma uses for the
// `cr2_read() as u8` cast (and `irq_mask(irq as u8)` in callers that
// hold the irq in a wider type).
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/trap/irq.vuma \
//         /tmp/irq.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/irq.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the 5 self-test checks all pass — 3 pushes
// succeed, count == 3 before dispatch_loop, count == 0 after
// dispatch_loop drains the ring).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The 32-entry lock-free IRQ ring buffer. Re-declared byte-identically
// to R2e's womb/kernel/trap/irq_ring.vuma. The asm trampoline is the
// single producer (push); the dispatcher (irq_dispatch_loop below) is
// the single consumer (pop). 268 bytes total (256 + 4 × 3). See
// irq_ring.vuma's header for the SPSC invariant + producer/consumer
// ordering.
layout IrqRing = {
    buf: [u8; 256],   // 32 × 8 bytes — u64 IRQ vectors (LE-packed)
    head: u32,        // read position (0..31, wraps via & 31)
    tail: u32,        // write position (0..31, wraps via & 31)
    count: u32,       // entries currently in buffer (0..32)
}

// The IRQ handler table. Re-declared byte-identically to K3d's pre-R2f
// irq.vuma. 256 entries (one per interrupt vector), each a u64 handler-
// function address (or 0 if unregistered). Stored as a flat [u8; 2048]
// array (256 × 8 bytes) because the codegen only lowers
// `state.array[idx]` correctly for [u8; N] arrays (see file header and
// pmm.vuma's "Why are PmmState fields [u8; N] instead of [u64; N]?" note
// for the full rationale). `count` tracks how many handlers are
// registered (incremented by register_irq, never decremented — there is
// no unregister_irq in this wave; K11 may add one if needed for driver
// hot-unplug).
layout IrqTable = {
    handlers: [u8; 2048],  // 256 * 8 bytes — u64 handler addr per IRQ
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern "C" trampolines — PIC mask/unmask/EOI (re-declared from K3a).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Re-declared byte-identically to K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma. K3a's file
// also declares idt_load and cr2_read; this file does NOT re-declare
// those because the IRQ subsystem doesn't use them (idt_load is called
// once during early boot by kmain, cr2_read is called by the page-fault
// handler). Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (returns 0, void calls no-op).
// K11 swaps these for real asm stubs registered in
// src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs.
extern "C" {
    // Mask an IRQ line at the 8259 PIC. `irq` is the IRQ number 0..15.
    // K11 stub: `mov al, 0xFF; out 0x21, al` (mask all — refined in K11
    // to per-IRQ bit-masking via `mov al, ~(1 << irq); out 0x21, al`).
    fn irq_mask(irq: u8);
    // Unmask an IRQ line at the PIC. K11 stub:
    // `mov al, 0xFE; out 0x21, al` (unmask IRQ 0 — refined in K11 to
    // per-IRQ bit-clearing). Pairs with irq_mask.
    fn irq_unmask(irq: u8);
    // Send End-Of-Interrupt to the PIC. Must be called at the end of
    // every hardware-IRQ handler so the PIC will deliver the next one.
    // K11 stub: `mov al, 0x20; out 0x20, al; ret` (non-specific EOI to
    // the master PIC's command port).
    fn pic_eoi(irq: u8);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IrqRing helpers (re-declared byte-identically to R2e's irq_ring.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// irq_ring_init / irq_ring_push / irq_ring_pop — copied verbatim from
// R2e so this file can be compiled standalone (no `import`). The
// self-test calls irq_ring_init + irq_ring_push + irq_ring_pop (the
// latter indirectly via irq_dispatch_loop) to exercise the ring buffer.

// Zero a caller-allocated IrqRing. Called once per IrqRing before any
// push or pop. Zeros the 256-byte buf (defensive — stale IRQ vectors
// from a prior use don't leak through if a future caller forgets to
// check count before popping), then sets head/tail/count to 0 (empty
// ring). Same explicit-zeroing shape as R2a's tf_init.
fn irq_ring_init(ring: State<IrqRing>) {
    ring.head = 0;
    ring.tail = 0;
    ring.count = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { ring.buf[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
}

// Enqueue one IRQ vector into the ring. Producer side. Returns 0 on
// success, -1 (written as `0 - 1` to avoid VUMA's negative-literal edge
// case — see waitq.vuma's header) if the ring is full.
//
// SPSC invariant: write vector BEFORE incrementing count, so the
// consumer (which checks count > 0 before reading) can never observe a
// stale vector. See irq_ring.vuma's header for the full ordering
// rationale.
fn irq_ring_push(ring: State<IrqRing>, vector: u64) -> i64 {
    if ring.count >= 32 {
        return 0 - 1;  // -1 (full)
    }
    // Write vector at tail position (8-byte LE pack).
    let off = ring.tail * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (vector >> sh) & 255;
        ring.buf[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    ring.tail = (ring.tail + 1) & 31;  // wrap around 32
    ring.count = ring.count + 1;
    return 0;
}

// Dequeue one IRQ vector from the ring. Consumer side. Returns the u64
// vector, or 9999 (the empty sentinel — out-of-range for any arch's
// IRQ vector space) if the ring is empty.
//
// SPSC invariant: read vector BEFORE decrementing count, so the producer
// (which checks count < 32 before writing) can never overwrite a slot
// the consumer is still reading.
fn irq_ring_pop(ring: State<IrqRing>) -> u64 {
    if ring.count == 0 {
        return 9999;  // empty sentinel
    }
    // Read vector at head position (8-byte LE unpack).
    let off = ring.head * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = ring.buf[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    ring.head = (ring.head + 1) & 31;  // wrap around 32
    ring.count = ring.count - 1;
    return v;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IrqTable byte-array pack/unpack helpers (kept verbatim from K3d).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte-identical in structure to pmm.vuma's pool_get_base / pool_set_base
// (K2a) and kmalloc.vuma's slab helpers (K2f): 8-iteration while loop,
// shift by i*8, mask 255 on writes, sum-of-shifted-bytes on reads. The
// only difference is the index type: pmm.vuma takes u32, this file takes
// u8 (and widens to u32 for the offset multiply — see "Why (irq as u32)
// * 8" above).

// Read the u64 handler address for IRQ `irq` from IrqTable.handlers.
// Returns 0 if no handler is registered for that IRQ (the slot is all
// zero bytes — register_irq hasn't been called for that IRQ yet, or the
// IRQ was never registered in the first place).
fn irq_get_handler(tbl: State<IrqTable>, irq: u8) -> u64 {
    let off = (irq as u32) * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.handlers[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 handler address for IRQ `irq` into IrqTable.handlers.
// Packs the 8 bytes little-endian (byte 0 = bits 7..0, byte 7 = bits
// 63..56). The mask-255 truncates each shifted byte to its low 8 bits
// (the (handler >> sh) >> 8 path would lose the high bits — same
// pattern as pmm.vuma's pool_set_base).
fn irq_set_handler(tbl: State<IrqTable>, irq: u8, handler: u64) {
    let off = (irq as u32) * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (handler >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.handlers[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IRQ lifecycle: register / dispatch_loop / enable / disable.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Register an IRQ handler. Kept verbatim from K3d.
//   1. Pack the handler address into tbl.handlers[irq*8..irq*8+8].
//   2. Increment tbl.count.
//   3. Call irq_unmask(irq) to clear the IRQ's mask bit at the PIC,
//      allowing the PIC to deliver that IRQ.
// In hosted mode, irq_unmask is __ffi_fallback_stub (no-op), so this is
// just a pack-and-count-increment. In bare-metal (K11), irq_unmask
// actually writes the PIC's mask register.
fn register_irq(tbl: State<IrqTable>, irq: u8, handler: u64) {
    irq_set_handler(tbl, irq, handler);
    tbl.count = tbl.count + 1;
    irq_unmask(irq);
}

// Drain the IRQ ring: pop vectors off the ring until it's empty, looking
// up each in the IrqTable and (if a handler is registered) sending EOI
// to the PIC. This is the R2f replacement for K3d's old single-IRQ
// `irq_dispatch(tbl, irq)` — the new model drains the entire ring in
// one call, so the trap return path invokes the dispatcher once and
// the loop handles all pending IRQs.
//
// The loop:
//   1. `v = irq_ring_pop(ring)` — pops the next vector (or 9999 if
//      empty).
//   2. If `v == 9999`: the ring is empty — return.
//   3. Else: narrow `v` to u8 (`v as u8`) for the IrqTable lookup + EOI
//      call (valid IRQ vectors are 0..255 / 0..63, all fit in a u8; the
//      9999 sentinel is caught at step 2 before the narrowing).
//   4. Look up the handler via `irq_get_handler(tbl, irq)`.
//   5. If non-zero: send EOI via `pic_eoi(irq)` (simplified — K11 adds
//      the real fn-pointer handler call before the EOI; VUMA has no
//      fn-pointer call today).
//   6. If zero (spurious IRQ): the contract's "simplified" path skips
//      the EOI — K11 will add the spurious-IRQ EOI (the 8259 PIC re-
//      delivers spurious IRQ 7 / 15 if you don't EOI them). For now,
//      this R2f stub matches the contract literally: "just call pic_eoi
//      if registered".
//   7. Repeat from (1).
//
// The loop terminates when the ring drains (step 2 sees 9999). Because
// the dispatcher is the single consumer (SPSC), no other code is
// popping the ring concurrently — the loop will drain exactly the
// vectors that were pushed before the dispatcher was called. New IRQs
// that arrive during the drain will be pushed by the asm trampoline
// (the producer) into the ring; if the drain is fast enough, those new
// IRQs are also drained in the same loop iteration; if the drain is
// slow, those new IRQs are left in the ring for the next dispatcher
// call (the trap return path will re-invoke irq_dispatch_loop on the
// next IRQ entry). This is the standard "drain-and-return" pattern.
//
// State<T> params: 2 (ring, tbl). The loop reads ring.head/ring.count/
// ring.buf[head*8..head*8+8] via irq_ring_pop, reads tbl.handlers via
// irq_get_handler, and calls pic_eoi (an extern "C" trampoline). The
// State<IrqRing> + State<IrqTable> are preserved across the pic_eoi
// extern call because they're passed as regular VUMA-fn borrows (NOT
// extern #[borrow] — only extern #[borrow] State<T> params are
// conservatively invalidated; pic_eoi takes only the u8 irq number, so
// neither State is at risk). The same pattern K3d's pre-R2f irq_dispatch
// used (it also called pic_eoi with a State<IrqTable> in scope).
fn irq_dispatch_loop(ring: State<IrqRing>, tbl: State<IrqTable>) {
    while true {
        let v = irq_ring_pop(ring);
        if v == 9999 {
            return;  // ring empty — drained
        }
        let irq = v as u8;
        let handler = irq_get_handler(tbl, irq);
        if handler != 0 {
            // Handler registered — send EOI (simplified; K11 adds the
            // real fn-pointer handler call before the EOI).
            pic_eoi(irq);
        }
        // Spurious IRQ (handler == 0): the contract's "simplified" path
        // skips the EOI. K11 will add the spurious-IRQ EOI (the 8259
        // PIC re-delivers spurious IRQ 7 / 15 if you don't EOI them).
    }
}

// Enable an IRQ (thin wrapper around irq_unmask). Kept verbatim from K3d.
// `irq` is the IRQ number 0..15 (the 8259 PIC only has 16 lines). K11
// may extend this to handle the slave PIC's IRQs 8..15 via the cascade.
fn enable_irq(irq: u8) {
    irq_unmask(irq);
}

// Disable an IRQ (thin wrapper around irq_mask). Kept verbatim from K3d.
// Pairs with enable_irq. Used by drivers to temporarily mask an IRQ
// during critical sections (e.g. while modifying a shared data structure
// the IRQ handler also touches).
fn disable_irq(irq: u8) {
    irq_mask(irq);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate an IrqRing + an IrqTable via state_new, zero-init both, then:
//   (1) Register a handler for IRQ 32 (the first PIC vector after the
//       8259 PIC's IRQ-base remap — on x86_64, this is the timer IRQ).
//       register_irq should pack the address into the table and
//       increment count.
//   (2) Push 3 vectors into the ring: 32 (registered), 33 (unregistered),
//       34 (unregistered). Each push returns 0 (the ring is empty when
//       we start, so all 3 pushes succeed).
//   (3) Verify ring.count == 3 after the 3 pushes (the producer side of
//       the SPSC invariant — count was incremented exactly 3 times).
//   (4) Call irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl) — drains the entire ring.
//       For vector 32: handler is registered (4096) → pic_eoi is called
//         (no-op in hosted mode).
//       For vectors 33 and 34: handler is 0 (unregistered) → no pic_eoi.
//   (5) Verify ring.count == 0 after dispatch_loop (the consumer side of
//       the SPSC invariant — the loop drained exactly 3 vectors, leaving
//       the ring empty). This is the contract's explicit DoD check.
// Returns 0 on success, 1..5 on the 5 individual check failures (so a
// future CI failure pinpoints the broken check).
//
// Per the R2f contract's "Use decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (32, 33, 34, 4096) — decimal
// avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension
// subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same
// rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied
// to rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let ring = state_new(IrqRing);
    let tbl = state_new(IrqTable);
    irq_ring_init(ring);
    tbl.count = 0;

    // Register a handler for IRQ 32 (timer, the first PIC vector). The
    // 4096 address catches any accidental u8-truncation in the pack/
    // unpack path (4096 > 255 — same rationale as K3a's rip=4096 check).
    register_irq(tbl, 32, 4096);

    // Push 3 vectors into the ring: 32 (registered), 33, 34 (both
    // unregistered — exercises both branches of irq_dispatch_loop's
    // handler != 0 check).
    let r1 = irq_ring_push(ring, 32);
    if r1 != 0 { return 1; }
    let r2 = irq_ring_push(ring, 33);
    if r2 != 0 { return 2; }
    let r3 = irq_ring_push(ring, 34);
    if r3 != 0 { return 3; }

    // Check: ring.count == 3 after the 3 pushes.
    if ring.count != 3 { return 4; }

    // Drain the ring via dispatch_loop. For vector 32: handler is
    // registered (4096) → pic_eoi is called (no-op in hosted). For
    // vectors 33 + 34: handler is 0 → no pic_eoi (the contract's
    // "simplified" path skips the spurious-IRQ EOI).
    irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl);

    // Check: ring.count == 0 after dispatch_loop drained the ring.
    // This is the contract's explicit DoD: "verify ring.count==0".
    if ring.count != 0 { return 5; }

    return 0;
}
